            "review_title": "Review at Pho Bo Hang Dong",
            "review_text": "David H\nRishon Lezion, Israel13 contributions\n0\nFour Generation Restaurateurs - and still the best !\nDec 2018 • Friends\nVietnam is a very friendly and fascinating country with many attractions, The Vietnamese kitchen and its gourmet dishes are among the magnets that bring growing numbers of tourists to the country. The Vietnamese soup - PHO (pronounced as FHOE) - is a unique dish combing various types of beef meat or fish, rice noodles, bean sprouts, coriander - cilantro leaves, lemon, and carob juice.\nThe legend places the best PHO in Saigon, South Vietnam and not at its northern parts like Hanoi the capital of Vietnam.\nBeing an admirer and lover of the Vietnamese PHO I have tested it around the globe. In Washington DC where a former Vietnamese journalist opened Pho restaurants at Arlington, Silver Springs and elsewhere, In Philadelphia, New York, and Paris, among the many that come to mind. I have also tried to make the Pho at home, for family and friends while using Vietnamese ingredients.\nThus I was shaken and surprised when I was directed by a Vietnamese acquaintance to a small Pho restaurant in the middle of Hanoi pictures old quarter. \nThe place is small, containing five, six tables who sit some 20 people. It is run by a wife and husband and grown-up son. According to the son who speaks English, the family owns and runs this Pho restaurant for the last sixty years and four generations. It is the same Pho that is served all over the country, eaten for Breakfast, Lunch, and Dinner - and yet it was different. \nIt was the best Pho I ever had and to make sure that I was not mistaken, we went back the next day for a second sampling. No doubt - it was the best PHO I ever had. And even now, weeks after our most recent visit to Hanoi, the taste of the Pho Bo 48 Hang Dong stays with me. Don't miss it.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nReview collected in partnership with this restaurant \nWritten January 15, 2019",
